# Chemical Reactions: Types, Rates, and Equilibrium

> Every change around you — rusting, breathing, digesting — is a
> chemical equation in motion. Learn the types, what speeds them up,
> and how reversible reactions settle into balance.

## 1. The Main Reaction Types

@diagram tree id="reaction-types-tree" layout="tree" aria="Five main types of chemical reactions"
  Reactions -> Combination
  Reactions -> Decomposition
  Reactions -> Displacement
  Reactions -> "Double displacement"
  Reactions -> "Redox (electron transfer)"
@/diagram

| Type | Pattern | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Combination | A + B → AB | CaO + H₂O → Ca(OH)₂ + heat |
| Decomposition | AB → A + B | CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂ (on heating) |
| Displacement | A + BC → AC + B | Fe + CuSO₄ → FeSO₄ + Cu |
| Double displacement | AB + CD → AD + CB | Na₂SO₄ + BaCl₂ → BaSO₄↓ + 2NaCl |
| Redox | electron transfer | ZnO + C → Zn + CO |

Decomposition comes in three flavours: **thermal** (heating CaCO₃),
**electrolytic** (an electric current splits water), and
**photolytic** (light drives 2AgBr → 2Ag + Br₂, the basis of
photography).

[info title="Spotting a precipitation"]
In double displacement, dissolved partners often swap to create
an insoluble solid — a **precipitate**, like the BaSO₄ above,
marked with a down-arrow.
[/info]

## 2. Balancing Equations

Matter is conserved, so atom counts must match on both sides.

[example]
Balance methane combustion, CH₄ + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O. Carbon: 1 = 1.
Hydrogen: 4 on the left needs 2 H₂O on the right. Oxygen: 2 + 2
(from CO₂ and 2H₂O) = 4, so 2 O₂ on the left:

CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O
[/example]

## 3. Exothermic vs Endothermic

| Type | Heat | ΔH sign | Everyday examples |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Exothermic | released | negative | respiration, burning, quicklime + water |
| Endothermic | absorbed | positive | photosynthesis, cooking, decomposing CaCO₃ |

## 4. Oxidation and Reduction

[definition]
**Oxidation** is loss of electrons and **reduction** is gain —
*OIL RIG*. The oxidizing agent is itself reduced; the reducing
agent is oxidized.
[/definition]

Take CuO + H₂ → Cu + H₂O: copper oxide is reduced (loses
oxygen) while hydrogen is oxidized (gains oxygen).

## 5. Corrosion and Rancidity

| Metal attacked | Product | Look |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Iron | rust, Fe₂O₃·xH₂O | reddish-brown, flaky |
| Silver | Ag₂S | black tarnish |
| Copper | CuCO₃·Cu(OH)₂ | green patina |

Corrosion is stalled by painting, greasing, galvanizing, or
alloying. **Rancidity** is the oxidation of fats and oils on
standing — checked by antioxidants (BHA, BHT), nitrogen flushing
of chip packets, and refrigeration.

## 6. Rates of Reaction

| Factor raised | Rate | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Concentration | up | more frequent collisions |
| Temperature | up | collisions faster and more energetic |
| Surface area | up | more exposed reaction sites |
| Catalyst present | up | lower activation energy; catalyst unchanged |

[example]
2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂ crawls along until a pinch of MnO₂ makes it
froth violently. In your saliva, the enzyme **amylase** catalyzes
starch digestion the same way — faster, but never consumed.
[/example]

## 7. Equilibrium and Le Chatelier

[definition]
**Chemical equilibrium** is the dynamic state of a reversible
reaction where forward and backward rates are equal, so
concentrations hold steady — but only in a closed system.
[/definition]

**Le Chatelier's principle**: disturb an equilibrium — by changing
concentration, pressure, or temperature — and the system shifts to
oppose the disturbance.

The Haber process, N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃ with ΔH = −92 kJ, shows every
lever:

| Disturbance | Shift | NH₃ yield |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Pressure up | right (2 mol gas vs 4) | up |
| Temperature up | left (backward is endothermic) | down |
| NH₃ removed | right | up |
| Iron catalyst added | none — just faster | unchanged |

[important title="The industrial compromise"]
Industry runs the Haber process near 450 °C and 200 atm over an
iron catalyst (promoted with Mo): high pressure for yield, moderate
temperature because low heat would be too slow.
[/important]

## 8. Quick Revision

| Question | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| Respiration is? | exothermic |
| Photosynthesis is? | endothermic |
| Rust formula? | Fe₂O₃·xH₂O |
| OIL RIG? | oxidation is loss, reduction is gain |
| Catalyst changes? | rate only, not the equilibrium position |
